<exercisegroup>
	<title>Using the Subspace Test </title>

	<introduction><p>
		In each of the following, check each part of the <xref ref="procedure-subspaces-concepts-test" text="title" /> for subcollection <m>W</m> inside vector space <m>V</m>.
		<ul>
			<li> If you think a part of the <xref ref="procedure-subspaces-concepts-test" text="title" /> is <em>true</em>, <alert>justify it without resorting to examples</alert>. </li>
			<li> If you think a part of the <xref ref="procedure-subspaces-concepts-test" text="title" /> is <em>false</em>, <alert>provide an explicit example that demonstrates it</alert>. </li>
		</ul>
		</p></introduction>

	<exercise>
		<statement><p> <m>V=\mathbb{R}^2</m>, <m>W=</m>  the subcollection of vectors that are orthogonal to <m>
			   \mathbf{v}=( 1,4)
			</m>.</p></statement>
		<answer><p> We can describe <m>W</m> as the subcollection of vectors <m>\mathbf{u}</m> where <m>\mathbf{u}\cdot \mathbf{v}=0</m>. Now we proceed with the subspace test:
<ol marker="(i)">
	<li>
		<p>
			We can demonstrate that <m>W</m> is nonempty using the zero vector <m> \zerovec=(0,0)</m>: <m>(0,0)\cdot(1,4)=0\cdot 1+0\cdot 4=0</m>.
		</p>
	</li>
		<li>
		<p>
			If <m>\uvec{u}_1</m> and <m>\uvec{u}_1</m> are both in <m>W</m>, then <m>\mathbf{u}_1\cdot \mathbf{v}=0</m> and <m>\mathbf{u}_2\cdot \mathbf{v}=0</m>. We can use <xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-dot-prod-algebra-distrib-right-over-left" text="distribution over the dot product" /> to show that <m>\mathbf{u}_1+\mathbf{u}_2</m> is also in <m>W</m>:
<me>
	(\mathbf{u}_1+\mathbf{u}_2)\cdot \uvec{v}=\uvec{u}_1\cdot\uvec{v}+\uvec{u}_2+\uvec{v}=0+0=0.
</me>
<m>\mathbf{u}_1+\mathbf{u}_2</m> is also orthogonal to <m>\mathbf{v}=( 1,4)</m>, so <m>\mathbf{u}_1+\mathbf{u}_2</m> is also in <m>W</m>.

		</p>
	</li>
	<li>
		<p>
			Consider a vector  <m>\uvec{u}</m> in <m>W</m>, and an arbitrary scalar <m>k</m>. We can use further <xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-dot-prod-algebra-scalar-left" text="properties of the dot product" /> to show that <m>k\uvec{u}</m> is also in <m>W</m>:
<me>
	(k\uvec{u})\cdot \uvec{v}=k(\uvec{u}\cdot\uvec{v})=k(0)=0.
</me>
<m>k\uvec{u}</m> is also orthogonal to <m>\mathbf{v}=( 1,4)</m>, so <m>k\uvec{u}</m> is also in <m>W</m>.

		</p>
	</li>
</ol>
All parts of the Subspace Test pass, so <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>.
		
		
		
		</p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement><p><m>V=\mathbb{R}^2</m>, <m>W=</m> the subcollection of vectors <m>
			    (x_1,x_2)
			</m>
			where
			<m>
			    x_{1}^2-x_{2}^2=0
			</m>.</p></statement>
		<answer><p>  </p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement><p><m>V=\mathbb{R}^2</m>, <m>W=</m> the subcollection of vectors <m>
			     (x_1,x_2)
			</m>
			where
			<m>
			    x_{1}+2x_{2}\geq 0.
			</m></p></statement>
		<answer><p>  </p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

		<exercise>
		<statement><p><m>V=M_{2\times 2}(\mathbb{R})</m>, <m>W=</m> the subcollection of matrices <m>A</m> where <m>A^2</m> is the zero matrix.
			</p></statement>
		<answer><p>  </p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

		<exercise>
		<statement><p><m>V=M_{2\times 2}(\mathbb{R})</m>, <m>W=</m> the subcollection of matrices <m>A</m> where <m>\det(A)\geq 0</m>.
			</p></statement>
		<answer><p>  </p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement><p><m>V=M_{2\times 2}(\mathbb{R})</m>, <m>W=</m> the subcollection of matrices <m>A</m> where <m>AX=XA</m> for <m>X=\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\2 &amp;4\end{bmatrix}</m>.
			</p></statement>
		<answer><p>  </p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement><p><m>V=M_{2\times 2}(\mathbb{R})</m>, <m>W=</m> the subcollection of upper triangular matrices.
			</p></statement>
		<answer><p>  </p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

		<exercise>
		<statement><p>
			<m>V=\poly_2(\R)</m>,  <m> W=</m> the subcollection of polynomials where <m>p(1)\geq 0</m> (recall: <m>p(1)</m> means to evaluate the polynomial <m>p(x)</m> at <m>x=1</m>).
			</p></statement>
		<answer><p>  </p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

			<exercise>
		<statement><p>
			<m>V=\poly_2(\R)</m>,  <m> W=</m> the subcollection of polynomials where <md>\int_{-1}^{1} p(x)dx=0.</md>
			</p></statement>
		<answer><p>  </p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement><p>
			<m>V=\poly_2(\R)</m>,  <m> W=</m> the subcollection of polynomials where <m>p'(0)+p'(1)=0</m> (recall: <m>p'(x)=\frac{d}{dx}p(x)</m> is the derivative of <m>p(x)</m>).
			</p></statement>
		<answer><p>  </p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
